{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1787188960584193002,"compiled_state":{"alias":"obese-dihedral-ermine","assignee":null,"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":4,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"93a08bc05d698309526852037739816b77f5cba005940853f50d074eb2902fac","event_uuid":"906738ae-6e6b-4286-a659-828ec76a3c39","position":{"commit_sha":"5525cf90c65c7a96d6b1733ddf0a7bccef62dc02","position":"1787188937033393001-906738ae-6e6b-4286-a659-828ec76a3c39"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"d37b8d37706425e09cbb8c595a9c2ef3dd0a9dc111418e285141c2be33469126","event_uuid":"14218f89-bdc1-4f82-9215-0e6a5e1499ce","position":{"commit_sha":"b7be29ec8682ce990914f3ab6c814c1e417ae1c1","position":"1787188942908701001-14218f89-bdc1-4f82-9215-0e6a5e1499ce"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"79428cc096edabdf35e510c98a615d27bb6356198a30fcfedbdfed1aee85c36c","event_uuid":"fdb6264b-afbb-473d-bb5c-b0b6aec87858","position":{"commit_sha":"20ce55dbf23c6a9df0e39eec94629b8166253edc","position":"1787188949786055001-fdb6264b-afbb-473d-bb5c-b0b6aec87858"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"},{"content_hash":"af8d1f9a38ffb89e9383a21f0b09b3c05ba0bfea8b51f8f213f4c621dd04e21f","event_uuid":"8667e6e3-c42a-4827-93b1-bff21d2c9369","position":{"commit_sha":"381f69ee906bfbbcac08399c10c5d203f053216c","position":"1787188960584193001-8667e6e3-c42a-4827-93b1-bff21d2c9369"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[],"created_at":1787188937033393001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"14218f89-bdc1-4f82-9215-0e6a5e1499ce","relation":"discovered_from","target_id":"d319-a094-4ee7-4549"},{"link_uuid":"fdb6264b-afbb-473d-bb5c-b0b6aec87858","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"c9f7-d6df-bce0-4b77"},{"link_uuid":"8667e6e3-c42a-4827-93b1-bff21d2c9369","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"6f2e-58a5-dfac-42f3"}],"description":"## DEFERRED — do not work this in the current session\nOperator decision 2026-08-19: this is a REVIEW-PROCESS follow-up, explicitly out of scope for the\nsession that found it. It is filed so the analysis is durable and a later session can pick it up\nwith the evidence already assembled. Do not claim it as part of unrelated work.\n\n## The miss\nBoth review gates passed Gerrit commit `8eadb4fbc5` (2026-08-05, \"Add shrink-only C901 complexity\nbaseline gate\", ticket `c9f7-d6df-bce0-4b77`) without detecting a contradiction contained ENTIRELY\nWITHIN THAT ONE DIFF.\n\nThe change introduced, in the same commit:\n- `scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py:64` — \"``stale>0`` alone is an allowed improvement (exit 0)\",\n  where `stale` is defined at `:61` as a function whose score DROPPED below its ceiling; and\n- `tests/unit/test_complexity_baseline.py:123` — `assert proc.stdout.strip() == f\"active={live}\n  new=0 increased=0 stale=0\"`, which forbids exactly that state.\n\nThe commit message states the intended contract twice — \"reductions and removed functions are\nreported as stale and allowed\" and \"wire --check into make lint so pre-commit and both CI lanes\ninherit the wrapper verdict\" — so the shipped behaviour contradicts the author's own stated design,\nnot merely an inferred one.\n\n## The bug it caused\n`dystopian-bardlike-basil` (d319-a094-4ee7-4549): reducing the complexity of a baselined function\npasses `make lint` and FAILS CI. Cost is already observable — Gerrit change 1928 lost a patchset to\na `Verified -1` across three ubuntu shards after going green locally, and its author then\ndeliberately reshaped the fix to be complexity-NEUTRAL (an unconditional helper call adds zero\ncyclomatic complexity) in order to avoid the gate. That is the gate steering a contributor away from\nthe improvement it exists to encourage.\n\n## What the gates actually did — measured, not assumed\n- **Plan review: 12 sidecars on ticket c9f7, BLOCK -> PASS, 20 distinct criteria fired**\n  (E1 E2 E4 E5 E6 F1 T1 T3 T4 T5b T5e T8 T9 G1G2 G6 G7 A1 COH P6 project.portability). The final\n  round surfaced one advisory, `P6` ac-quality. Thorough by any measure.\n- **Code review RAN** (the gate landed 2026-08-05; the earliest code-review sidecar in the store is\n  2026-07-10, so the capability predates it) and found FIVE real findings on change\n  `I95acf3a92b891212a42b36e72ae5fd50ce12d6ca`: a module-level `atomic_write` import triggering\n  `rebar/__init__.py` side effects; `build_symbol_index` computing `name_col` differently from\n  Ruff's C901 location column; an untested column-disambiguation path; an untested `_run_check`\n  error branch; and a dropped supply-chain note that the gate parses version-sensitive Ruff output.\n\nEvery one of those is about whether the CODE is correct and tested. None is about whether the\nPOLICY is self-consistent. The review was competent; it was aimed at a different question.\n\n## Why it was invisible — the class, not the instance\n1. **Neither artifact is wrong alone.** The tolerance is correct. The assertion was ALSO correct at\n   authoring time — the commit says the baseline \"is generated from the live census\", so it really\n   was in sync. Two true statements; the defect is only in their interaction.\n2. **The harm is TEMPORAL.** At review time `stale=0` held and would keep holding until some FUTURE\n   contributor reduced complexity in a baselined function. Neither gate simulates a future\n   contributor's workflow against a newly-introduced gate.\n3. **A plan cannot express it.** The plan said reductions are allowed — coherent and correct. The\n   contradiction is emergent from two implementation artifacts, so plan review had nothing to object\n   to across twelve rounds.\n4. **No criterion is aimed at it.** The built-in code-review registry (a11y, api-compat,\n   conflict-markers, db-migrations, deletion-impact, docs, error-handling, high-critical-security,\n   i18n, iac, llm-prompts, performance, public-exposure-without-auth, regression, scope-intent,\n   secret-detection, security, supply-chain, surface-parity, tests) is uniformly about the code\n   artifact. Nothing asks whether a newly-introduced gate leaves a workable path for the contributor\n   it will block.\n5. **It was IN SCOPE.** Both files were in the same diff. This is not a case where the reviewer\n   could not see the other half — it is a genuine miss of a cross-file interaction in a review that\n   caught five subtler mechanical issues.\n\nThe generalisable signature: **artifacts individually correct, jointly contradictory, consequence\ndeferred to a future change.** Every reviewer instinct — read the file, check the logic, check the\ntests — passes on each half independently.\n\n## Constraint on any proposed remedy — read before designing one\nA superficially similar criterion was REJECTED by the operator on 2026-08-19: `ci-gate-compat`\n(\"new hard-fail gates must grandfather in-flight branches\", ticket `unaroused-undead-ibisbill`,\nGerrit 1919 abandoned). The rejection reasoning was sound and still stands: when a gate fails an\nin-flight branch, the remedy is to rebase and fix the actual failure, so no compatibility mechanism\nis warranted. **Do not re-propose that.**\n\nThe distinction this ticket rests on: there, the contributor CAN fix it by fixing their code. Here\nthey cannot — their code is correct and better, the gate blocks the improvement, and the only\nsanctioned remedy (`--update-stale`) is maintenance-only. The open question is not \"should gates\nhave compat ramps\" but \"should a gate be required to leave ANY sanctioned path for the legitimate\ncase it blocks\". Whether that deserves a criterion, a plan-review question, or nothing at all is\nundecided and is what this ticket is for.\n\n## Suggested starting points (not a decision)\n- Whether this class is better served by a criterion, or by feeding it to the existing escaped-bug\n  analysis practice (`cranial-goodly-seahog`), which exists to consume exactly this material.\n- Whether any OTHER shipped gate has the same shape — a stated tolerance contradicted elsewhere, or\n  a hard-fail with no contributor path. A survey would establish whether this is a one-off or a\n  pattern, and that answer should drive the remedy's weight.\n- Note that item 4 above is a factual claim about today's registry and should be re-verified when\n  this is picked up; the registry changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A verdict is recorded on whether this class warrants a new review criterion, an addition to an\n      existing one, or no mechanism — with the reasoning, so the decision is not re-litigated.\n- [ ] If a mechanism is adopted, it is demonstrably distinct from the rejected `ci-gate-compat`\n      criterion, and that distinction is stated in its ticket.\n- [ ] A survey of shipped gates for the same shape (stated tolerance contradicted elsewhere, or\n      hard-fail with no sanctioned contributor path) is recorded, so the remedy is sized to evidence\n      rather than to this single instance.\n","env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[["relates_to","6f2e-58a5-dfac-42f3"],["relates_to","c9f7-d6df-bce0-4b77"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":3,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"7f9f-05f4-796a-4c63","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Review-gate miss: a cross-file policy contradiction shipped through 12 plan-review rounds and a 5-finding code review","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["906738ae-6e6b-4286-a659-828ec76a3c39","14218f89-bdc1-4f82-9215-0e6a5e1499ce","fdb6264b-afbb-473d-bb5c-b0b6aec87858","8667e6e3-c42a-4827-93b1-bff21d2c9369"]},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1787188960584193002,"uuid":"0debf1c0-339b-4a2f-b66b-73d3606b1ba8"}